WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:06.506
♪ ♪

00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:09.475
ALEX: I'm headed down
to my new plot of land,

00:00:09.508 --> 00:00:11.677
Sipsy comes running out of
the woods down there and

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:13.313
he's got this in his mouth.

00:00:17.783 --> 00:00:20.553
It's a, I don't know
if he caught it,

00:00:20.586 --> 00:00:22.855
it doesn't have
any marks on it.

00:00:22.888 --> 00:00:24.590
I have no idea how it died,

00:00:24.623 --> 00:00:25.958
I, maybe he
just grabbed it and

00:00:25.991 --> 00:00:28.895
throttled it and
that's a successful hunt.

00:00:29.428 --> 00:00:31.964
So Sipsy just literally
killed us dinner.

00:00:31.997 --> 00:00:33.900
Come on Sipsy, come on.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.169
Hey. No, no, no, no, uh-uh.

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:38.071
You'll get him, don't worry.

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:41.307
This definitely seems
like a good omen.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.309
I'm not superstitious
but, you know, if I was,

00:00:43.342 --> 00:00:47.414
I would definitely
think this was a sign
of good things to come.

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:50.450
That's my boy.

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:02.629
ALEX: Come on, Sipsy.
Time to go.

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:06.399
My new land is, is located
quite a bit closer to an area

00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:08.868
where I know I
can hunt beavers.

00:01:08.901 --> 00:01:10.736
There's moose, maybe ducks,

00:01:10.769 --> 00:01:13.006
and generally
just good hunting.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:17.110
If I can build like
a small kind of hunting
slash trapping cabin,

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:19.545
you know, just a real
basic shelter that could make

00:01:19.578 --> 00:01:22.816
it easier for me to
hunt throughout the winter.

00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:27.086
 NARRATOR: Alex Javor's main
 hunting grounds are two hours

00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:29.989
 from his A-frame
 cabin by foot.

00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:33.693
 A dangerous journey in
 the harsh Alaskan winters.

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:37.029
 In order to protect
 himself from being
 stranded along the way,

00:01:37.062 --> 00:01:40.099
 Alex plans to build a
 safe haven shelter halfway

00:01:40.132 --> 00:01:43.036
 from his cabin to
 the hunting grounds.

00:01:46.872 --> 00:01:50.443
ALEX: This is kind of
what I'm looking for. Yeah.

00:01:53.345 --> 00:01:55.281
I guess you just might call
it artistic or something.

00:01:55.314 --> 00:01:58.751
I like to kind of,
like, put it where, like,

00:01:58.784 --> 00:02:02.588
kind of where the land
wants it anyway, you know,

00:02:02.621 --> 00:02:06.058
so we have, like, this
kind of this natural clearing.

00:02:06.091 --> 00:02:08.361
You come in here,
clear out some of these trees,

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:12.331
kind of clear the area
out a little bit and use
those same trees to build

00:02:12.364 --> 00:02:15.835
uh, kinda, just like
a really simple, um,

00:02:15.868 --> 00:02:17.737
shelter basically
or something,

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:21.774
just something I
can build a fire in maybe
put a small camp stove in,

00:02:21.807 --> 00:02:23.342
and I'm gonna get back.

00:02:23.375 --> 00:02:26.679
Tomorrow's gonna be a long
day a lot of hard work.

00:02:33.285 --> 00:02:35.321
BRENT: Conditions in
Alaska, are they ever ideal?

00:02:35.354 --> 00:02:38.224
Uh, it all depends on
your point of view and
what your expectations are.

00:02:38.257 --> 00:02:39.926
You can't have
any expectations,

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:42.762
you just kinda gotta deal
with whatever is thrown at you.

00:02:43.362 --> 00:02:45.832
Ideal conditions
are all relative.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.809
BRENT: This is generally
where I have my firewood and,

00:02:56.842 --> 00:02:59.913
uh, as you can see
there's not a lot happening,

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:03.115
it's a pretty
sad firewood stash.

00:03:03.148 --> 00:03:06.686
It's 20 degrees out and
winter has come and there's no

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:10.456
firewood put away, so it
means we go cut firewood.

00:03:11.356 --> 00:03:15.028
Firewood is key
mainly because that's how we
heat two of our buildings,

00:03:15.894 --> 00:03:17.330
and then on top of that,

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:19.565
probably the most important
thing is we cook fish every

00:03:19.598 --> 00:03:22.735
day for the dogs and we
use wood to boil the fish.

00:03:22.768 --> 00:03:25.738
It's an essential thing
for just feeding the dogs.

00:03:28.507 --> 00:03:30.643
We have a lot of
cutting ahead of us.

00:03:30.676 --> 00:03:33.479
It's a beautiful day so we're
gonna take advantage of that.

00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:34.780
It just got to be done.

00:03:34.813 --> 00:03:37.984
You've got to make it a
top priority starting now.

00:03:38.784 --> 00:03:42.188
Let's see if we can
find some firewood.

00:03:42.221 --> 00:03:45.358
All right, well, this
is the trail that goes up.

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:49.595
So Ida's still visiting her
family in Sweden so I'm out

00:03:49.628 --> 00:03:52.531
here by myself definitely
missing a piece of the puzzle,

00:03:52.564 --> 00:03:54.533
Ida takes care of a lot of
stuff around here and makes my

00:03:54.566 --> 00:03:55.968
life a lot easier,

00:03:56.001 --> 00:03:57.837
so I'm definitely missing her.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:00.339
But I have to work.

00:04:00.372 --> 00:04:02.441
This is the killing
fields right here,

00:04:02.474 --> 00:04:07.046
this is where all the trees
are laying down on the ground.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:14.387
Yeah, I think this
is my path here.

00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:20.994
(chainsaw revs)

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:27.400
The lifestyle here
is basically nonstop.

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:29.902
Even though I work way more
than the average person does

00:04:29.935 --> 00:04:33.106
in civilization, it's
totally worth it to me.

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:35.808
Out here you just figure
out a way to do it and

00:04:35.841 --> 00:04:38.711
I think that's what really
draws me to this lifestyle.

00:04:38.744 --> 00:04:40.346
You don't call anybody.

00:04:40.379 --> 00:04:43.716
You don't ask for help,
you just get it done.

00:04:46.251 --> 00:04:48.754
There's no give up out here.

00:04:48.787 --> 00:04:51.557
I think that's
the general trail.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:53.493
Looks decent enough.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.128
We'll do a little
trial run with the machine

00:04:56.161 --> 00:04:59.465
without a load and we'll,
uh, give it a go here.

00:05:02.835 --> 00:05:05.271
I'm gonna really have to
regulate how big a loads that

00:05:05.304 --> 00:05:08.074
I can take, with a heavy
load it can flip the machine,

00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:12.078
it can do all kinds of things.

00:05:12.344 --> 00:05:14.046
It's the lack of
deep snow is our biggest

00:05:14.079 --> 00:05:16.415
challenge right now.

00:05:16.448 --> 00:05:17.817
We can't really change
that so we've just got

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:20.386
to kind of work around it.

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:23.623
Pretty smooth sailing,
we got a trail in.

00:05:23.923 --> 00:05:26.025
It works.

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:28.661
Progress, one step at a time.

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:39.706
KALEB: It's almost better
than taking them to school,

00:05:40.006 --> 00:05:44.410
learning real life
application lessons that they

00:05:44.443 --> 00:05:47.213
can carry into adulthood.

00:05:50.549 --> 00:05:52.151
KALEB: All right.
You guys ready to go?

00:05:52.184 --> 00:05:54.721
GILBERT: Uh-huh.

00:05:56.722 --> 00:06:00.660
KALEB: Gilbert, over here.
Ellie, come on.

00:06:04.763 --> 00:06:09.201
My kids are the next
generation and it's really

00:06:09.234 --> 00:06:13.372
important for me as a father
to teach them all the skills

00:06:13.405 --> 00:06:16.542
that I know for
living out in the bush.

00:06:17.976 --> 00:06:20.379
Today we are heading out
to a beaver pond that's next

00:06:20.412 --> 00:06:22.381
to the McCarthy Road.

00:06:22.414 --> 00:06:24.650
These beavers have been
cutting down trees,

00:06:24.683 --> 00:06:26.352
dropping them across the road.

00:06:26.385 --> 00:06:28.921
So, we need to go
harvest a couple of them

00:06:28.954 --> 00:06:33.092
and take it
as an opportunity to teach
my kids some new skills.

00:06:35.327 --> 00:06:37.062
You guys ready to
get some beaver bait?

00:06:37.095 --> 00:06:39.098
GILBERT: Uh-huh.

00:06:39.131 --> 00:06:42.101
KALEB: Do you know what
beavers like to eat guys?

00:06:42.134 --> 00:06:43.369
GILBERT: Trees.

00:06:43.402 --> 00:06:45.438
KALEB: Green trees.

00:06:45.471 --> 00:06:47.039
You know what we
call Cotton Woods?

00:06:47.072 --> 00:06:49.442
GILBERT: Huh?
ELOVIE: What?

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:52.311
KALEB: Beaver candy, because
they like it so much.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:57.416
I think it's important to
teach my children everything

00:06:57.449 --> 00:06:59.218
that I've learned up
to this point and,

00:06:59.251 --> 00:07:01.987
and hopefully more
as I get older.

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:04.790
That way someday, if they
choose to live this lifestyle,

00:07:04.823 --> 00:07:08.327
they will be well-equipped
to live in a place like this.

00:07:10.896 --> 00:07:14.133
As a kid I learned by just
getting out and doing it.

00:07:18.036 --> 00:07:22.308
I believe it's important
especially children living in

00:07:22.341 --> 00:07:26.179
rural areas like we are,
that they learn life skills.

00:07:28.046 --> 00:07:31.951
All right, guys, let's
go trap us some beavers.

00:07:39.157 --> 00:07:40.993
MICHAEL: You jump
on a snow machine and those

00:07:41.026 --> 00:07:43.963
things will fail you
time and time again.

00:07:43.996 --> 00:07:45.498
It's soul less.

00:07:45.531 --> 00:07:49.001
You jump on an old-fashioned
freight sled with a bunch of

00:07:49.034 --> 00:07:52.472
malamutes that look
like a picture from 100
years ago in Alaska.

00:07:53.071 --> 00:07:54.740
That's got soul.

00:08:03.348 --> 00:08:06.219
MICHAEL: I call this
the "15-Gallon Shuffle".

00:08:07.619 --> 00:08:09.388
Five gallons of food.

00:08:10.288 --> 00:08:12.558
You go back around
five gallons of water.

00:08:14.259 --> 00:08:17.597
You go back around
one more time and pick
up five gallons of poop.

00:08:19.898 --> 00:08:22.434
The past week of dog
training by dog sled,

00:08:22.467 --> 00:08:24.737
I had about a foot of
snow packed to work with.

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:27.373
Now, all the snow was melted.

00:08:27.406 --> 00:08:30.910
I can't run my
sleds no more but their
training has to continue.

00:08:31.543 --> 00:08:33.746
But I've got a winter
caribou hunt coming up,

00:08:33.779 --> 00:08:37.349
so now is the time to switch
over to a fat tire bike and

00:08:37.382 --> 00:08:40.052
just hope and wait
for more snow.

00:08:41.253 --> 00:08:44.223
Chores are all done now
it's time get on the trail
and check it

00:08:44.256 --> 00:08:47.092
after this bad
windstorm and this warm weather.

00:08:47.125 --> 00:08:49.662
So when I clear a trail
and I hike around,

00:08:49.695 --> 00:08:53.332
I like to bring my
retired sled dog Nina.

00:08:54.266 --> 00:08:58.337
She is 11 years old and
she is the grandmother to all

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:00.673
these puppies down the hill,

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:03.776
which are going to be
great sled dogs this year.

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:10.049
Nina. Nina. Nina.

00:09:13.318 --> 00:09:16.322
She's going, uh, deaf.

00:09:18.323 --> 00:09:22.494
During this warm weather
the winds came, rains came,

00:09:22.527 --> 00:09:24.129
trees were breaking.

00:09:24.162 --> 00:09:27.266
I'm going to go ahead and do
a sweep of my local trails and

00:09:27.299 --> 00:09:29.869
then jump on a bike
and start training up.

00:09:30.736 --> 00:09:33.405
Winters can get rough,
it's dark, it's cold,

00:09:33.438 --> 00:09:36.408
and a lot of people
just get hold up.

00:09:36.441 --> 00:09:38.444
For me, these dogs,
they get me out,

00:09:38.477 --> 00:09:39.979
they get me moving around,

00:09:40.012 --> 00:09:42.815
and their excitement
rubs off onto me.

00:09:43.415 --> 00:09:46.152
This season belongs to them.

00:09:48.086 --> 00:09:51.958
Here I've got a birch
tree that's about falling
down over the trail,

00:09:52.858 --> 00:09:55.594
so I'm just going to go
ahead and cut this one down.

00:09:55.627 --> 00:09:58.431
Just to be on the safe side.

00:09:58.664 --> 00:10:01.100
Any sort of wind when
this wood gets brittle,

00:10:01.133 --> 00:10:04.970
it could snap and fall
in the coldest weather with

00:10:05.003 --> 00:10:07.272
just a little bit of wind.

00:10:07.305 --> 00:10:10.909
So, I'm going to try to
cut this with a hand saw.

00:10:10.942 --> 00:10:12.978
Come here, Nina.

00:10:13.011 --> 00:10:14.947
I need you to sit, right here.

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:18.050
Come here. Sit.

00:10:18.583 --> 00:10:22.188
She's so deaf I have to
give her hand signals.

00:10:23.889 --> 00:10:25.424
Since this is leaning,

00:10:25.457 --> 00:10:27.526
it does have the
possibility to kick back.

00:10:27.559 --> 00:10:30.763
It never feels like a safe
thing to do when I have to

00:10:30.796 --> 00:10:35.300
clear stuff leaning like
this but might as well get

00:10:35.333 --> 00:10:38.204
to work because this
is going to be awhile.

00:10:47.913 --> 00:10:52.317
(sawing)

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:53.919
(wood cracking)

00:10:53.952 --> 00:10:56.388
Come here, Nina.
Come on. Nina. Hey.

00:10:56.421 --> 00:10:58.324
Nina. Go! Go!

00:11:02.661 --> 00:11:05.831
MICHAEL: Come here, Nina.
Come on. Nina. Hey.

00:11:05.864 --> 00:11:08.634
Nina. Go! Go!

00:11:16.341 --> 00:11:18.544
Nee-nee, you're grounded.

00:11:18.577 --> 00:11:21.380
No video games.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:23.982
That thing fell quick.

00:11:24.015 --> 00:11:26.518
It was leaning so strong.

00:11:26.551 --> 00:11:28.921
I only got about
halfway in before it,

00:11:28.954 --> 00:11:33.625
it snapped and it's a likely
deal that this would have fell

00:11:33.658 --> 00:11:35.594
over the trail sometime.

00:11:35.627 --> 00:11:38.631
And I don't want it to fall
on the trail when I'm on it.

00:11:42.501 --> 00:11:45.504
It's good doing this physical
labor with hand tools.

00:11:45.537 --> 00:11:49.542
I don't even use
an internal combustion
for really anything.

00:11:50.342 --> 00:11:52.845
When you get out here it's
a little harsh at first.

00:11:52.878 --> 00:11:55.547
Splitting wood by hand,
hauling water by hand,

00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:58.918
running a dog team instead
of running a snow mobile.

00:12:00.519 --> 00:12:04.490
It's a game for me to not
spend money on your things

00:12:04.523 --> 00:12:06.558
that you want me to buy.

00:12:06.591 --> 00:12:09.261
That's the game for me.

00:12:13.064 --> 00:12:17.136
All right, we've
got this big birch tree
cleared off the trail.

00:12:17.169 --> 00:12:18.570
Time to keep going,

00:12:18.603 --> 00:12:21.207
I got four more
miles of trail to do.

00:12:22.541 --> 00:12:24.910
I'll sleep good tonight.

00:12:34.019 --> 00:12:35.721
PRODUCER: What it's like
being a father bringing your

00:12:35.754 --> 00:12:38.390
two kids out and teaching
them some new lessons?

00:12:41.193 --> 00:12:43.129
KALEB: Stressful.

00:12:47.532 --> 00:12:50.002
KALEB: All right,
guys, let's go.

00:12:53.872 --> 00:12:56.975
Bringing out the kids
on the ice is stressful.

00:12:57.008 --> 00:13:01.614
Safety is number one on
an expedition like this.

00:13:03.915 --> 00:13:06.218
I don't want my kids
to fall in the water.

00:13:06.251 --> 00:13:08.320
I don't want them to get
wet which could put them

00:13:08.353 --> 00:13:11.023
at the risk of hypothermia.

00:13:13.491 --> 00:13:15.093
Are you guys getting wet?

00:13:15.126 --> 00:13:16.428
GILBERT: No.

00:13:16.461 --> 00:13:18.530
KALEB: Good.

00:13:18.563 --> 00:13:20.332
You know its zero
degrees outside,

00:13:20.365 --> 00:13:24.270
it's very easy
to get cold and not be
able to recover from that.

00:13:29.541 --> 00:13:32.578
GILBERT: You will get cold.

00:13:33.011 --> 00:13:35.247
Is it cold?

00:13:37.148 --> 00:13:38.484
KALEB: Ah.

00:13:38.717 --> 00:13:40.686
When I was about the
same age as Gilbert,

00:13:40.719 --> 00:13:42.788
on one of the sets we were
doing when I was a little kid,

00:13:42.821 --> 00:13:44.890
I actually fell in the hole.

00:13:47.158 --> 00:13:49.027
My father reached down
and grabbed me before

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:51.530
I went under the ice.

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:57.703
And so, I've always been
really careful since then not

00:13:57.736 --> 00:14:00.806
to be too close to the
edge of the water there.

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:03.508
Stay away from the hole, guys,

00:14:03.541 --> 00:14:05.143
you don't want
to fall into that.

00:14:05.176 --> 00:14:07.145
Once we cut a hole in the ice,

00:14:07.178 --> 00:14:09.782
we stick the cottonwood
pole down in the center.

00:14:10.015 --> 00:14:13.085
You put that down and they
just, they go nuts over it.

00:14:14.219 --> 00:14:19.224
We'll shove it down in the mud
about as far as it will go.

00:14:19.257 --> 00:14:23.128
Then we have two dead cross
poles that we stick over it.

00:14:24.462 --> 00:14:26.932
We lash the cottonwood
pole to the dead poles and

00:14:26.965 --> 00:14:29.735
then we hang snares
off the dead poles.

00:14:30.202 --> 00:14:32.671
And that ought
to stay in place.

00:14:32.971 --> 00:14:36.041
All right, guys,
lets grab the snares and
start hanging them in there.

00:14:36.074 --> 00:14:41.180
GILBERT: Okay.
the pole got (inaudible).

00:14:42.347 --> 00:14:44.116
ELOVIE: (inaudible) Oh!

00:14:44.382 --> 00:14:46.852
KALEB: Oh, hey,
we snared the dog.

00:14:46.885 --> 00:14:49.154
We've got you, Piper.

00:14:49.254 --> 00:14:51.924
(Elovie shrieks)

00:14:52.691 --> 00:14:54.860
Okay, that was really
loud and screechy,

00:14:54.893 --> 00:14:56.228
but that was pretty funny.

00:14:56.261 --> 00:14:57.729
GILBERT: Uh-huh.

00:14:57.762 --> 00:15:00.399
KALEB: I'm hoping that all
of these little skills that I

00:15:00.432 --> 00:15:05.437
teach them will set them up
for a great future if someday

00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:07.806
they choose to
live out in the bush.

00:15:08.873 --> 00:15:11.143
ELOVIE: Shovel me, dad.

00:15:11.977 --> 00:15:14.146
Shovel me, dad.

00:15:14.412 --> 00:15:17.082
KALEB: It's entirely their
choice but it really is a

00:15:17.115 --> 00:15:19.318
pretty good life
out in the woods.

00:15:24.756 --> 00:15:26.658
BRENT: It's basically
constant work.

00:15:26.691 --> 00:15:28.360
It's a lot of problem solving,

00:15:28.393 --> 00:15:30.595
a lot of improvising
and making things up,

00:15:30.628 --> 00:15:33.666
it's the way of life
that I love and thrive at.

00:15:39.971 --> 00:15:42.708
BRENT: Okay. So, yeah,
this is the stuff

00:15:42.741 --> 00:15:47.613
Unfortunately, it's
all buried in the snow
but lots of good firewood.

00:15:54.219 --> 00:15:56.788
A super heavy load going down
that trail would not be a very

00:15:56.821 --> 00:15:59.591
good idea mainly just because
we don't have enough snow.

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:04.162
I'm gonna want to
take as much as I possibly

00:16:04.195 --> 00:16:06.398
can down with every load.

00:16:06.431 --> 00:16:09.034
I know that I don't want to
be there all day long so we're

00:16:09.067 --> 00:16:11.170
going to find a happy medium.

00:16:19.878 --> 00:16:21.713
If you hurt
yourself out here,

00:16:21.746 --> 00:16:24.149
you're a long ways from help.

00:16:24.983 --> 00:16:26.551
If you cut your finger off,

00:16:26.584 --> 00:16:28.720
you're probably not going
to have that finger anymore.

00:16:28.753 --> 00:16:30.889
It's, it's just
the way it is out here.

00:16:30.922 --> 00:16:35.294
Well, so much for
a conservative load
but it's worth a shot.

00:16:38.296 --> 00:16:42.634
Worst thing is it overtakes a
snow machine and flips over.

00:16:44.002 --> 00:16:47.339
You've got to live
on the edge but not
that close to the edge.

00:16:52.577 --> 00:16:55.014
I think we'll be okay.

00:16:58.283 --> 00:17:00.185
Famous last words.

00:17:09.994 --> 00:17:16.301
♪ ♪

00:17:17.368 --> 00:17:20.706
Whoa! Oh (bleep).

00:17:29.414 --> 00:17:31.383
BRENT: Oh (bleep).

00:17:35.253 --> 00:17:37.189
We made it!

00:17:40.091 --> 00:17:42.861
Feels awesome
to have the first load
of wood off the mountain.

00:17:42.894 --> 00:17:45.230
It's totally doable
and once we get more snow,

00:17:45.263 --> 00:17:47.466
it'll be,
I don't think, a problem at all.

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:52.037
It swings around quite a bit
but we made it down no problem.

00:17:52.070 --> 00:17:54.506
I don't think
I'd want to haul much
heavier of a load than,

00:17:54.539 --> 00:17:56.608
than that one.

00:17:56.641 --> 00:17:58.510
Living out here, living
this kind of lifestyle,

00:17:58.543 --> 00:18:01.380
efficiency is super important.

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:04.516
You just got to try and make
the best out of every trip,

00:18:04.549 --> 00:18:06.585
wherever you're
going around here.

00:18:06.818 --> 00:18:08.687
We're gonna be able to have
a nice warm cabin tonight.

00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:11.023
So it's a start.

00:18:13.158 --> 00:18:15.360
This will last us a few days.

00:18:17.295 --> 00:18:19.831
We'll get a few more
loads so this pile will grow.

00:18:19.864 --> 00:18:21.900
And then it'll shrink.

00:18:22.767 --> 00:18:25.137
And then it'll
grow again, hopefully.

00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:28.240
More firewood to get.

00:18:37.715 --> 00:18:39.918
ALEX: Yeah. Some people,
especially when you're, um,

00:18:39.951 --> 00:18:42.687
kind of miserable and
exhausted and cold and wet,

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:44.322
they might want
to know like,

00:18:44.355 --> 00:18:46.024
"Don't you, like,
miss hot showers and

00:18:46.057 --> 00:18:47.659
warm rooms and
stuff like that now?"

00:18:47.692 --> 00:18:49.694
And, honestly I don't at all.

00:18:49.727 --> 00:18:51.630
I don't think about it.

00:18:52.564 --> 00:18:55.400
Um, that just comfort and
convenience just doesn't mean

00:18:55.433 --> 00:18:57.369
anything to me.

00:19:05.810 --> 00:19:08.413
 NARRATOR: After finding
 a suitable location,

00:19:08.446 --> 00:19:11.082
 Alex must now harvest
 enough viable trees to

00:19:11.115 --> 00:19:13.352
 build his hunting shelter.

00:19:14.386 --> 00:19:17.289
 But with daylight and
 temperatures quickly fading,

00:19:17.322 --> 00:19:20.893
 it's imperative that
 Alex use every hour wisely

00:19:21.159 --> 00:19:24.596
 in order to get his cabin
 built before it's too late.

00:19:26.931 --> 00:19:28.533
ALEX: This is going to
be a good amount of work.

00:19:28.566 --> 00:19:30.936
I think the fastest I
can do this would be, uh,

00:19:30.969 --> 00:19:33.104
probably three days,
realistically.

00:19:33.137 --> 00:19:35.173
I'm gonna make this
guy fall this way,

00:19:35.206 --> 00:19:37.676
just 'cause it's clear path.

00:19:37.709 --> 00:19:40.145
No obstructions.

00:19:40.411 --> 00:19:42.914
But I'm gonna try to get it
done in two days but they're

00:19:42.947 --> 00:19:45.117
gonna be really busy days.

00:19:57.362 --> 00:19:59.130
So I should have
everything I need in here.

00:19:59.163 --> 00:20:02.400
My goal is to get about 36
good logs for the walls and

00:20:02.433 --> 00:20:04.469
then I can use pretty
much any dinky little

00:20:04.502 --> 00:20:06.338
ones for the roof.

00:20:06.371 --> 00:20:09.808
A log like this might be
fine for the, for the wall.

00:20:11.242 --> 00:20:13.912
Definitely be
good for the roof.

00:20:14.946 --> 00:20:18.416
One of the big challenges I'm
facing when I do this work is,

00:20:18.449 --> 00:20:19.718
it's pretty warm,

00:20:19.751 --> 00:20:21.753
which you'd think
would be nice but, uh,

00:20:21.786 --> 00:20:23.154
it means all the snow,

00:20:23.187 --> 00:20:25.056
it falls on me
and then it melts

00:20:25.089 --> 00:20:26.591
and then I get really wet.

00:20:26.624 --> 00:20:28.727
So then if I need to
stop and take a break,

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:30.262
because I'm getting tired,

00:20:30.295 --> 00:20:33.131
uh, I get really
cold pretty quickly.

00:20:33.164 --> 00:20:35.900
You know within an hour
you could have hypothermia.

00:20:35.933 --> 00:20:39.871
So the only thing to do
is keep working, keep going.

00:20:40.371 --> 00:20:42.507
It's not good to push too
far into being exhausted when

00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:44.676
you're doing dangerous
work like this.

00:20:44.709 --> 00:20:46.544
Uh, pushing to the point
where my hands are going

00:20:46.577 --> 00:20:48.981
to slip and I'm gonna
slice myself with my own ax.

00:20:53.351 --> 00:20:56.321
Still got a long,
long ways to go.

00:20:56.354 --> 00:20:58.323
I don't know if I'll be
able to cut 36 down today,

00:20:58.356 --> 00:20:59.524
we'll see.

00:20:59.557 --> 00:21:02.194
But we'll just keep working.

00:21:02.460 --> 00:21:05.196
It's really easy to become
lazy especially in modern life.

00:21:05.229 --> 00:21:07.732
Everything's designed
for our convenience.

00:21:07.765 --> 00:21:10.368
Every time we
increase technology to
benefit our convenience,

00:21:10.401 --> 00:21:13.672
we kind of get a
little more disconnected
from the earth.

00:21:13.705 --> 00:21:16.574
Just by going back
50 years and cutting my wood

00:21:16.607 --> 00:21:18.143
with a saw and an ax.

00:21:18.176 --> 00:21:19.644
Woo, it's heavy.

00:21:19.677 --> 00:21:22.414
It just gives me
a lot more appreciation
for modern lifestyle.

00:21:22.447 --> 00:21:25.517
Just so it's a little
bit uncomfortable,

00:21:25.550 --> 00:21:27.819
you know it's, it's work.

00:21:30.088 --> 00:21:32.557
But I feel like it keeps
you from getting lazy or

00:21:32.590 --> 00:21:34.760
taking things for granted.

00:21:39.530 --> 00:21:41.566
Woo.

00:21:44.435 --> 00:21:46.905
That's enough
work for one day, I think.

00:21:46.938 --> 00:21:50.442
I'll probably need some
more of the, maybe not.

00:21:51.042 --> 00:21:52.844
I might have enough.

00:21:52.877 --> 00:21:54.479
We'll see.

00:21:54.512 --> 00:21:58.450
Well, I better get
home before I get cold.

00:21:58.783 --> 00:22:01.920
So. I'm feeling
pretty exhausted.

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:03.922
Today I've made a huge mess.

00:22:03.955 --> 00:22:05.890
I'll have to clean it up.

00:22:05.923 --> 00:22:08.293
Tomorrow the real
struggle begins.

00:22:17.268 --> 00:22:21.272
MICHAEL: Hunting with a
team of Alaskan Malamutes,

00:22:21.305 --> 00:22:22.974
it's just priceless.

00:22:23.007 --> 00:22:26.845
I couldn't picture
my life now without
hunting caribou by dog team.

00:22:34.552 --> 00:22:37.189
MICHAEL: All right,
bike feels good.

00:22:37.755 --> 00:22:40.092
Time to flip out.

00:22:40.325 --> 00:22:42.060
Today's a good day.

00:22:42.093 --> 00:22:43.461
Got the trail clear.

00:22:43.494 --> 00:22:45.864
Got the studded
tires on this thing.

00:22:45.897 --> 00:22:49.201
And I'll just keep running
dogs until they're all run.

00:22:51.569 --> 00:22:53.805
All right, I'm gonna
start with the two rudest,

00:22:53.838 --> 00:22:56.842
biggest, strongest
jerks in the lot.

00:22:57.742 --> 00:22:58.810
The wheel dogs.

00:22:58.843 --> 00:23:01.780
The ones that don't
listen well, Snoop and Kyran.

00:23:06.150 --> 00:23:08.453
I gotta get out of
here in one piece.

00:23:08.486 --> 00:23:11.156
My last run with
Snoop and Kyran,

00:23:11.189 --> 00:23:15.594
they were so excited and they
were so amped up they slammed
me down onto the ground,

00:23:16.461 --> 00:23:18.696
they ripped the
chain off the bike.

00:23:18.729 --> 00:23:21.766
Being three or four times
the size of a race dog,

00:23:21.799 --> 00:23:26.004
if they slam their harnesses
when I least expect it,

00:23:26.037 --> 00:23:28.406
I'm going flying off a sled.

00:23:28.439 --> 00:23:31.777
It's rough running dogs
but it humbles you too.

00:23:33.277 --> 00:23:36.047
You gotta get a good
hold on that boy.

00:23:36.481 --> 00:23:40.585
(grunting)

00:23:41.285 --> 00:23:43.088
Ah (bleep).

00:23:44.822 --> 00:23:47.192
He's too strong
for his own good.

00:23:47.926 --> 00:23:50.395
It's been about three
days since they've ran.

00:23:50.428 --> 00:23:52.030
So they're gonna
be really amped up.

00:23:52.063 --> 00:23:53.865
The anxiety is gonna be high.

00:23:53.898 --> 00:23:56.067
The dogs are gonna be
screamin', howlin', barkin'.

00:23:58.369 --> 00:24:02.273
It's a mess but
if I can get out of the
yard upright on this bike,

00:24:02.306 --> 00:24:04.443
I'll be good to go.

00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:09.281
Stay. Stay. Stay.

00:24:14.352 --> 00:24:16.388
Stay!

00:24:16.588 --> 00:24:18.523
Whoa. Whoa.

00:24:20.057 --> 00:24:21.326
Holy (bleep)!

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:27.432
MICHAEL: Get up there.

00:24:27.465 --> 00:24:29.668
Whoa. Whoa.

00:24:31.769 --> 00:24:33.405
Holy (bleep).

00:24:38.776 --> 00:24:41.679
Good boys. Good boys.

00:24:41.712 --> 00:24:44.582
All right,
we got out the door.

00:24:44.615 --> 00:24:48.086
My knee is sore,
but I'll be all right.

00:24:49.086 --> 00:24:51.289
These sled dogs,
these Alaskan malamutes,

00:24:51.322 --> 00:24:54.592
there's something about
traveling with these dogs that

00:24:54.625 --> 00:24:58.830
seems more real to
me than the automobile
on the asphalt road.

00:25:00.231 --> 00:25:02.867
All right, the dogs'
gates look good.

00:25:02.900 --> 00:25:06.137
We're going at about a
seven mile an hour pace.

00:25:06.170 --> 00:25:07.572
They're both in unison.

00:25:07.605 --> 00:25:10.341
They're not runnin' too
hard, they're not lungin'.

00:25:10.374 --> 00:25:12.944
They're just hoofin' it.

00:25:15.346 --> 00:25:18.450
This time of the year, to
not have snow is so weird.

00:25:18.850 --> 00:25:22.554
But the training continues
by fat tire bike.

00:25:22.854 --> 00:25:24.455
Human power.

00:25:24.488 --> 00:25:26.858
Dog power.

00:25:28.159 --> 00:25:30.729
Manzo malamutes.

00:25:31.096 --> 00:25:34.799
A little bike ride
in the birch forest.

00:25:41.172 --> 00:25:42.740
BRENT: People pay, you know,

00:25:42.773 --> 00:25:45.009
tons and tons of money at
the gym or, or, you know,

00:25:45.042 --> 00:25:47.912
the trainers or whatever
to keep them in shape,

00:25:47.945 --> 00:25:50.415
but out here just normal
life keeps you in shape and,

00:25:50.448 --> 00:25:52.450
and cuttin' wood is
just part of normal life.

00:25:56.387 --> 00:25:59.858
BRENT: Yeah. Beautiful day,
nice and chilly.

00:26:01.826 --> 00:26:04.195
Time to buck up some wood.

00:26:06.530 --> 00:26:10.168
Wealth here is a big stack
of firewood, lots of dog food,

00:26:10.201 --> 00:26:12.036
and warm temperatures.

00:26:12.069 --> 00:26:14.272
But we have to deal with
whatever comes at us.

00:26:14.305 --> 00:26:17.542
And wealth is definitely,
it's another relative term.

00:26:18.009 --> 00:26:21.346
You have to be able to take
the punches and get back up

00:26:21.379 --> 00:26:23.014
and keep going forward.

00:26:27.084 --> 00:26:31.222
Technology allows us to
do all the things

00:26:32.356 --> 00:26:35.326
that we have to do in
a more efficient way.

00:26:35.359 --> 00:26:38.597
But it's definitely a good
workout splitting wood.

00:26:40.798 --> 00:26:43.001
If I make it
to my mid-70s here,

00:26:43.034 --> 00:26:44.769
I'll be a happy man.

00:26:50.441 --> 00:26:52.043
If I can't split
this piece of wood,

00:26:52.076 --> 00:26:54.212
I'm never gonna make
it here to my mid-70s.

00:26:56.981 --> 00:26:58.883
Just the whole purpose
of cutting firewood,

00:26:58.916 --> 00:27:01.119
it's gonna warm ya four or
five times before you actually

00:27:01.152 --> 00:27:03.288
get it to the wood stove.

00:27:06.624 --> 00:27:08.459
It's definitely
therapeutic at times.

00:27:08.492 --> 00:27:11.095
I'm kind of checked out
away from normal society and

00:27:11.128 --> 00:27:13.765
normal everyday
nine-to-five life.

00:27:13.798 --> 00:27:16.167
Not knowing what
the next challenge you're
going to have to overcome,

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:17.802
and what the next
thing is going to be.

00:27:17.835 --> 00:27:21.473
The uncertainty
of this life I think is
what I enjoy most about it.

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:25.243
That's it.

00:27:25.276 --> 00:27:27.545
Last cuts for this pile.

00:27:27.811 --> 00:27:29.113
Ah.

00:27:29.146 --> 00:27:31.616
Yeah, well, got
a little supply.

00:27:31.649 --> 00:27:34.819
It looks good to have a
wood pile around here.

00:27:34.852 --> 00:27:37.522
Oh, yeah.

00:27:39.590 --> 00:27:42.360
I see a warm
cabin right there.

00:27:47.398 --> 00:27:49.467
KALEB: I've always enjoyed
being outside from the time

00:27:49.500 --> 00:27:50.968
I was a little kid.

00:27:51.001 --> 00:27:54.672
And so, it's great seeing
my kids learning to enjoy the

00:27:54.705 --> 00:27:57.875
outdoors and the
cold and the snow and

00:27:57.908 --> 00:28:02.814
just everything about being
out in the fresh clean air.

00:28:06.984 --> 00:28:10.455
KALEB: Who's excited that we
get to check beaver sets on

00:28:10.488 --> 00:28:12.423
Ellie's fourth birthday?

00:28:12.456 --> 00:28:13.858
GILBERT: Me.

00:28:13.891 --> 00:28:15.993
KALEB: That's pretty cool,
isn't it?

00:28:16.026 --> 00:28:19.330
It's kind a special day
because it's Elovie's birthday.

00:28:19.496 --> 00:28:21.933
It's pretty unique to be
able to take my daughter out and

00:28:21.966 --> 00:28:24.268
go check beaver sets.

00:28:24.301 --> 00:28:26.270
She seems pretty
jazzed about it.

00:28:26.303 --> 00:28:28.539
And I don't know very many
four-year-old girls who go

00:28:28.572 --> 00:28:31.643
beaver trapping with their
fathers on their birthday.

00:28:33.177 --> 00:28:35.246
Guys, I see a
pole's tipped over.

00:28:35.279 --> 00:28:36.748
You know what that means?

00:28:36.781 --> 00:28:38.716
GILBERT: Trapped a beaver.

00:28:38.749 --> 00:28:41.386
KALEB: Means a beaver's
been chewing on it.

00:28:42.019 --> 00:28:44.422
All right, let's go check it.

00:28:49.059 --> 00:28:52.830
All right, I'm checking the
ice here with my ice spud to

00:28:52.863 --> 00:28:55.567
make sure it's
safe to walk here.

00:28:57.201 --> 00:29:00.271
There is no guarantees
that we'll be successful.

00:29:00.304 --> 00:29:03.608
Huh. Well, let's take a
peek and see what's in there.

00:29:05.342 --> 00:29:08.146
There's a 50/50 shot that we
can come back with food or not

00:29:08.179 --> 00:29:11.215
come back with food.

00:29:11.248 --> 00:29:14.352
There's definitely a sense
of anticipation and just not

00:29:14.385 --> 00:29:16.521
knowing what's gonna happen.

00:29:16.554 --> 00:29:18.990
I love trapping season.

00:29:31.869 --> 00:29:34.138
Oh, jeez.

00:29:34.171 --> 00:29:35.873
GILBERT: Daddy,
what are those?

00:29:35.906 --> 00:29:38.143
KALEB: I have no
idea what those are.

00:29:43.848 --> 00:29:45.850
KALEB: Oh, jeez.

00:29:46.317 --> 00:29:48.486
GILBERT: Daddy,
what are those?

00:29:48.519 --> 00:29:50.822
KALEB: I have no
idea what those are.

00:29:53.524 --> 00:29:58.163
Never seen bugs like
that in a pond ever.

00:29:58.630 --> 00:30:00.364
Like, that is crazy.

00:30:00.397 --> 00:30:02.266
ELOVIE: And they're not dead.

00:30:02.299 --> 00:30:04.235
GILBERT: Uh-huh.

00:30:04.268 --> 00:30:06.404
KALEB: I don't see any beaver.

00:30:06.437 --> 00:30:09.107
It appears like we
might have got skunked.

00:30:09.340 --> 00:30:12.143
Not a single beaver,
just a bunch of bugs.

00:30:12.510 --> 00:30:15.446
Well, let's pull it up
and just take a look.

00:30:20.618 --> 00:30:22.186
Look at that.

00:30:22.219 --> 00:30:24.856
Right down at the bottom.

00:30:24.889 --> 00:30:27.158
So our snares were right
at the top of the ice and

00:30:27.191 --> 00:30:29.927
they hung down about
a little over a foot.

00:30:29.960 --> 00:30:32.163
So somewhere in this range.

00:30:32.196 --> 00:30:35.533
That's where
our snares were and the
beaver was clean down here.

00:30:35.566 --> 00:30:38.469
So he came to the set and he
took our bait but he took it

00:30:38.502 --> 00:30:42.006
where we didn't have
any snares to catch him.

00:30:42.039 --> 00:30:44.609
He done outsmarted us.

00:30:45.943 --> 00:30:48.646
It's a great lesson for the
kids to learn that you can't

00:30:48.679 --> 00:30:51.215
necessarily be successful
every single time you

00:30:51.248 --> 00:30:52.783
go into the woods.

00:30:52.816 --> 00:30:55.553
It's pretty disappointing
but just getting out with them,

00:30:55.586 --> 00:30:57.655
whether we're
successful or not,

00:30:57.688 --> 00:31:00.258
is what makes this
trip a success.

00:31:00.291 --> 00:31:02.126
GILBERT: Daddy,
see the bugs I got out?

00:31:02.159 --> 00:31:05.496
KALEB: Yup. You just
reduced the bug population
by several million

00:31:05.529 --> 00:31:07.098
next summer, probably.

00:31:07.131 --> 00:31:10.068
Ready to go for a
little sled ride?

00:31:10.268 --> 00:31:11.369
Come on, Ellie.

00:31:11.402 --> 00:31:12.670
ELOVIE: It's on me.

00:31:12.703 --> 00:31:14.839
KALEB: Don't walk in the hole.

00:31:15.940 --> 00:31:18.242
We might not have gotten
a beaver today but it's

00:31:18.275 --> 00:31:19.944
Ellie's birthday and so, uh,

00:31:19.977 --> 00:31:22.146
we need to have some fun
with her and Gilbert.

00:31:22.179 --> 00:31:24.148
Ready?

00:31:29.386 --> 00:31:31.355
(laughing)

00:31:31.388 --> 00:31:33.758
Our playground out
here is limitless.

00:31:33.791 --> 00:31:39.097
We live in the middle of
a 13-million-acre park.

00:31:42.800 --> 00:31:45.002
We may not have swing
sets in our park,

00:31:45.035 --> 00:31:47.571
but we do have some pretty
awesome hills to go sledding

00:31:47.604 --> 00:31:50.775
on and plenty of snow
to make that happen.

00:32:02.419 --> 00:32:06.124
MICHAEL: It's been
a learning process for
those dogs and myself,

00:32:06.424 --> 00:32:08.025
but it's in their blood.

00:32:08.058 --> 00:32:10.461
They, they know what
to do from hundreds,

00:32:10.494 --> 00:32:12.230
if not thousands
of generations.

00:32:14.665 --> 00:32:18.002
MICHAEL: You want to go again?
You want to go again?

00:32:18.035 --> 00:32:19.537
(barking and howling)

00:32:19.570 --> 00:32:22.140
You can't knock a dog
for high motivation.

00:32:22.773 --> 00:32:25.309
Time to finish up the
final run of the day.

00:32:25.342 --> 00:32:27.078
Lead dog Mau.

00:32:27.111 --> 00:32:28.713
Little female Cleo.

00:32:28.746 --> 00:32:31.282
And one of their puppies, Red.

00:32:32.649 --> 00:32:35.219
See the guy chewing
on his chain?

00:32:35.252 --> 00:32:38.623
We're gonna run that
goofball over there.

00:32:38.856 --> 00:32:40.691
Let's do this.

00:32:40.724 --> 00:32:45.063
Next up is hookin' up
two veterans and one pup.

00:32:45.863 --> 00:32:48.532
And the pup's lucky because
it's his mom and dad.

00:32:48.565 --> 00:32:51.302
So they'll be on
the same brainwave.

00:32:51.335 --> 00:32:53.037
Hopefully, theoretically.

00:32:56.540 --> 00:32:59.777
This is impossible to put
a harness on this guy.

00:33:01.278 --> 00:33:06.417
Training puppies is
probably one of the most
frustrating things ever.

00:33:07.551 --> 00:33:09.820
People think
puppies are cute and
I just look at 'em like,

00:33:09.853 --> 00:33:12.290
"Oh, my God, I've got
a lot of work ahead of me."

00:33:13.657 --> 00:33:16.394
This is actually,
it makes for a good sled dog.

00:33:16.427 --> 00:33:20.264
The most spastic one of
the bunch is also the
hardest working pup.

00:33:23.100 --> 00:33:26.003
And maybe someday this
guy might be a lead dog.

00:33:26.036 --> 00:33:28.106
You never know.

00:33:30.107 --> 00:33:33.144
I need you guys to teach
this boy well, alright?

00:33:37.848 --> 00:33:40.684
Might be hectic with three
dogs on a bike but I need two

00:33:40.717 --> 00:33:44.522
veterans to teach a dog
what's right from wrong.

00:33:44.555 --> 00:33:47.725
But still, puppies do all
kinds of crazy stuff so this

00:33:47.758 --> 00:33:50.228
might be a hectic run.

00:33:52.229 --> 00:33:54.866
Go get 'em. Yah, yah, yah!

00:33:59.603 --> 00:34:01.472
Nice to get a little
bit of snow coming back.

00:34:01.505 --> 00:34:03.140
It's promising.

00:34:03.173 --> 00:34:06.978
Maybe after a couple
or more good storms
I'll be back on the sled.

00:34:08.512 --> 00:34:11.015
We've got about two miles in.

00:34:11.048 --> 00:34:12.650
We got to keep him moving.

00:34:12.683 --> 00:34:15.753
The more he runs the
calmer he'll get.

00:34:20.057 --> 00:34:22.626
This puppy has good focus.

00:34:22.659 --> 00:34:27.298
It's very rare for a dog to
be this young and to be this

00:34:27.331 --> 00:34:29.867
focused on being a sled dog.

00:34:30.868 --> 00:34:33.537
And every time
I run this puppy,

00:34:33.570 --> 00:34:37.508
he's gonna get a little bit
more focused and he's gonna

00:34:37.541 --> 00:34:39.877
put all his energy
to good use.

00:34:43.213 --> 00:34:45.216
Good dogs.

00:34:46.450 --> 00:34:49.753
I find working with these
animals just as therapeutic as

00:34:49.786 --> 00:34:52.923
it is for the animals
running the miles.

00:34:52.956 --> 00:34:56.193
As an Iraq war
veteran with PTSD,

00:34:56.226 --> 00:35:00.865
it really helps me cope
with a lot of my trauma and

00:35:01.298 --> 00:35:05.703
it really excites me watching
puppies grow up and be strong,

00:35:05.736 --> 00:35:08.606
confident courageous sled dogs.

00:35:09.072 --> 00:35:11.242
And I love it.

00:35:14.978 --> 00:35:16.614
ALEX: I think to live
out here you have to be

00:35:16.647 --> 00:35:18.816
pretty stubborn and
bullheaded sometimes.

00:35:19.683 --> 00:35:21.919
Uh, just, uh, just to
get through, you know,

00:35:21.952 --> 00:35:23.988
tough challenges.

00:35:24.021 --> 00:35:26.724
At the same time if
you go too far,

00:35:26.757 --> 00:35:30.327
if you're not able to back out
into a wider perspective,

00:35:30.360 --> 00:35:32.396
you can get yourself
in trouble that way.

00:35:39.236 --> 00:35:41.639
ALEX: Think I'll need an ax.

00:35:42.139 --> 00:35:44.008
This morning, uh,
I'm feeling good.

00:35:44.041 --> 00:35:47.044
I'm trying to get this cabin
done before we get into the

00:35:47.077 --> 00:35:48.879
depths of winter.

00:35:48.912 --> 00:35:50.581
But now it's crunch time.

00:35:50.614 --> 00:35:53.384
I have to finish it
and get it done quick.

00:35:53.417 --> 00:35:55.319
Can get my hammer.

00:35:55.352 --> 00:35:58.489
Uh, maybe I don't need that.

00:35:59.356 --> 00:36:02.026
Today's task is gonna be
to start notching the logs,

00:36:02.059 --> 00:36:05.596
stacking them up,
building the walls.

00:36:05.896 --> 00:36:07.431
With the chainsaw and ATV,

00:36:07.464 --> 00:36:09.033
I could just blast
through the work and

00:36:09.066 --> 00:36:10.834
get it done quick and, uh,

00:36:10.867 --> 00:36:13.471
if the result is your
only goal, that's cool.

00:36:14.871 --> 00:36:18.042
Uh, for me it's the journey,
not the destination.

00:36:19.810 --> 00:36:21.645
I'm gonna say the
stove is the kind of the

00:36:21.678 --> 00:36:23.681
back end of the cabin.

00:36:23.714 --> 00:36:28.886
So the back wall would be here
and then walls on either side.

00:36:29.920 --> 00:36:32.223
Just keep it real simple.

00:36:33.123 --> 00:36:35.926
I'm using just
a really classic log

00:36:35.959 --> 00:36:37.728
cabin building technique.

00:36:37.761 --> 00:36:40.231
You can just think of
it like a rectangle.

00:36:42.532 --> 00:36:45.069
A rectangle that's designed
for me to crawl inside,

00:36:45.102 --> 00:36:46.870
sleep in, and stay warm.

00:36:46.903 --> 00:36:49.306
So it needs to room for
me and a wood-stove and a

00:36:49.339 --> 00:36:52.109
little extra room
just to store some gear
or something like that.

00:36:54.211 --> 00:36:56.247
This is a good technique
because it doesn't require

00:36:56.280 --> 00:36:58.482
really any specialized tools.

00:36:58.515 --> 00:37:01.619
It's just built very
simply by stacking up logs,

00:37:01.652 --> 00:37:04.588
uh, and then you
put kind of a U-shaped
notch in them so they

00:37:04.621 --> 00:37:07.959
fit over each other and nest
on top of another log neatly.

00:37:08.793 --> 00:37:11.161
Let me just see
what that's like.

00:37:11.194 --> 00:37:14.232
Huh. Good
enough for government work.

00:37:16.633 --> 00:37:18.135
I'm just gonna be
eye-balling it,

00:37:18.168 --> 00:37:21.172
going real fast even
if it's not air-tight.

00:37:22.039 --> 00:37:23.140
That's fine.

00:37:23.173 --> 00:37:25.743
It's just a survival
shelter really.

00:37:28.412 --> 00:37:30.781
Hopefully, I can finish up.

00:37:31.348 --> 00:37:33.117
My joints hurt.

00:37:33.150 --> 00:37:35.352
I have no strength in my arms.

00:37:35.385 --> 00:37:38.122
Uh, gripping the
ax feels unsafe.

00:37:38.422 --> 00:37:41.759
You know, it feels
like I could drop it.

00:37:41.959 --> 00:37:44.462
I'm just exhausted.

00:37:49.399 --> 00:37:51.769
Uh, we're just putting
on the final logs.

00:37:51.802 --> 00:37:53.137
That'll work.

00:37:53.170 --> 00:37:55.339
And then I'll start laying
the roof in-between.

00:37:55.372 --> 00:37:59.777
I'll, before I do
the roof though I
gotta frame in the door,

00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:01.712
cut that out.

00:38:01.745 --> 00:38:04.415
Yeah, I'm pretty,
pretty exhausted.

00:38:04.448 --> 00:38:06.817
Uh, yeah, we're getting there.

00:38:06.850 --> 00:38:09.120
Just about there.

00:38:11.688 --> 00:38:13.324
(bleep).

00:38:13.357 --> 00:38:15.726
Left my hammer at the cabin.

00:38:15.759 --> 00:38:18.462
I'll try to make use of my ax.

00:38:18.929 --> 00:38:22.232
(hammering)

00:38:22.265 --> 00:38:24.302
(groans)

00:38:42.219 --> 00:38:43.788
Ah, (bleep) piece of (bleep).

00:38:49.826 --> 00:38:52.396
(hammering)

00:38:52.429 --> 00:38:54.999
ALEX: (bleep)
piece of (bleep).

00:38:55.332 --> 00:38:56.667
Um, I gotta get a hammer.

00:38:56.700 --> 00:38:57.735
I can't do it like this.

00:38:57.768 --> 00:39:00.170
I can't drive (bleep)
500 nails like that.

00:39:00.203 --> 00:39:02.606
That'll take a (bleep) year.

00:39:02.639 --> 00:39:06.644
Um, left my hammer
at the cabin.

00:39:08.412 --> 00:39:11.014
Trying to hammer nails
with an ax is, uh,

00:39:11.047 --> 00:39:13.984
exercise in frustration.

00:39:14.217 --> 00:39:18.555
So I think I'm
gonna head up there real
quick and get my hammer.

00:39:18.588 --> 00:39:20.458
It'll be worth it.

00:39:21.391 --> 00:39:22.893
In the modern world,

00:39:22.926 --> 00:39:24.561
you'd say time
is money but out here,

00:39:24.594 --> 00:39:25.662
time's all you got.

00:39:25.695 --> 00:39:27.564
So you don't want to waste it.

00:39:27.597 --> 00:39:29.366
All this comes at a cost.

00:39:29.399 --> 00:39:32.302
And it costs me a couple
of hours of daylight.

00:39:32.335 --> 00:39:33.971
There's never, never
enough this time of year,

00:39:34.004 --> 00:39:38.309
but I'll try to make use
of every hour I've got left.

00:39:40.911 --> 00:39:44.248
(hammering)

00:39:44.548 --> 00:39:47.017
There's only a couple
hours of daylight.

00:39:47.050 --> 00:39:49.219
I don't prefer to work in the
dark so I'm gonna get straight

00:39:49.252 --> 00:39:51.288
to it and work fast and
furious and try to get as much

00:39:51.321 --> 00:39:53.691
work done as I can, uh,
before it gets dark.

00:39:56.426 --> 00:39:58.328
I call this my framing.

00:39:58.361 --> 00:40:02.266
Um, just hammer it in
to hold these guys.

00:40:02.299 --> 00:40:04.835
Then I'll saw it right
in-between and then we can

00:40:04.868 --> 00:40:06.971
lay the roof, I think.

00:40:11.174 --> 00:40:15.079
If you don't got patience,
you're gonna get it,

00:40:16.880 --> 00:40:20.384
'cause this is
gonna take a while.

00:40:23.820 --> 00:40:27.124
(saw grinding)

00:40:34.965 --> 00:40:36.500
What do you think, Sipsy?

00:40:36.533 --> 00:40:37.634
Do you want to go in there?

00:40:37.667 --> 00:40:39.236
It looks like a
safe cozy spot,

00:40:39.269 --> 00:40:41.672
where you're protected
on three sides.

00:40:41.905 --> 00:40:44.008
Kind of like a den?

00:40:44.708 --> 00:40:48.312
He's making a, there you go.

00:40:48.679 --> 00:40:51.248
If Sipsy approves, I'm happy.

00:40:51.715 --> 00:40:54.351
That's the only
one that matters.

00:40:56.653 --> 00:40:58.055
The roof's really simple.

00:40:58.088 --> 00:41:00.123
I just take all the thinner
trees that weren't big enough

00:41:00.156 --> 00:41:03.894
for walls and layer
them across the top.

00:41:04.828 --> 00:41:08.098
On top of all that,
I'm just gonna layer
all these extra

00:41:08.131 --> 00:41:12.336
spruce prawns that we
had around that will
act as insulation.

00:41:13.236 --> 00:41:15.706
It's not particularly
fire safe,

00:41:15.739 --> 00:41:17.608
and it wouldn't
keep the rain out,

00:41:17.641 --> 00:41:20.978
but for a winter hunting
cabin, it'll get the job done.

00:41:24.848 --> 00:41:26.183
I finished the cabin.

00:41:26.216 --> 00:41:28.285
Mostly I feel relief
that the work's done,

00:41:28.318 --> 00:41:31.054
but I also am excited to,
uh, test it out, you know,

00:41:31.087 --> 00:41:32.523
kind of like a new toy.

00:41:32.556 --> 00:41:34.758
And, uh, see if it
burns down immediately.

00:41:43.233 --> 00:41:47.704
♪ ♪

00:41:47.737 --> 00:41:50.073
 ♪ They come with 
 bows and arrows ♪

00:41:50.106 --> 00:41:52.610
 ♪ They come with ♪♪

00:41:53.377 --> 00:41:55.880
ALEX: It's working,
it's working.

00:41:56.113 --> 00:41:57.281
This is right.

00:41:57.314 --> 00:41:59.082
Yeah, I'm actually really
looking forward to go hunting

00:41:59.115 --> 00:42:00.551
because I can
definitely use the meat.

00:42:00.584 --> 00:42:02.219
Coming back and spending
the night out here,

00:42:02.252 --> 00:42:05.656
it's gonna be,
it's gonna be cool.

00:42:06.189 --> 00:42:10.261
 ♪ We are the hunted, 
 we are the chosen ones ♪♪

00:42:10.661 --> 00:42:12.696
BRENT: Being out here,
being out with the dogs or

00:42:12.729 --> 00:42:15.633
just being out cuttin'
firewood just feels free.

00:42:16.633 --> 00:42:17.634
You guys hungry?

00:42:17.667 --> 00:42:19.136
You guys want
something to eat?

00:42:19.169 --> 00:42:21.805
It just takes a lot of work
but it's all worth it because

00:42:21.838 --> 00:42:24.341
of that feeling of freedom.

00:42:24.374 --> 00:42:27.044
Forward progress, every day.
That's all you gotta do,

00:42:27.077 --> 00:42:28.279
is make a
little forward progress.

00:42:31.214 --> 00:42:33.350
(barking)

00:42:33.383 --> 00:42:34.585
MICHAEL: You want a
little bit of fat?

00:42:34.618 --> 00:42:35.686
You gotta sit.

00:42:35.719 --> 00:42:38.255
Sit. Good boy.
You did good today.

00:42:38.989 --> 00:42:42.927
These sled dogs, they want
to go and they want to explore.

00:42:45.128 --> 00:42:47.898
They re-invigorate my spirit.

00:42:48.798 --> 00:42:51.668
That's why I'm a
dog musher for life.

00:42:51.701 --> 00:42:55.205
Until I'm an old man and
my body can't move anymore,

00:42:55.238 --> 00:42:58.242
I'll be a dog musher
until the end.

00:42:59.542 --> 00:43:01.044
KALEB: Life for the Rowlands,

00:43:01.077 --> 00:43:02.846
out here in the
middle of the wilderness,

00:43:02.879 --> 00:43:04.314
is just beginning.

00:43:04.347 --> 00:43:07.217
One day at a time,
one lesson at a time.

00:43:07.250 --> 00:43:09.186
BRITTNEY: Ta-da!

00:43:09.219 --> 00:43:10.354
GILBERT: Wow.

00:43:10.387 --> 00:43:12.422
BRITTNEY: Look what I made.

00:43:12.455 --> 00:43:16.326
I think what Kaleb
and I have out here is
pretty special and unique.

00:43:16.359 --> 00:43:18.929
 ♪ Happy birthday to you. ♪♪

00:43:18.962 --> 00:43:21.198
BRITTNEY: And I really love
that we were able to do that

00:43:21.231 --> 00:43:25.402
together and we've
created such a lovely
place to call our home.

00:43:27.637 --> 00:43:33.544
♪ If we are the hunted
then we are the chosen ones. ♪♪

00:43:34.511 --> 00:43:37.314
BRENT: Every day is a
knock down drag out fight.

00:43:37.347 --> 00:43:39.182
But it's what
we signed up for.

00:43:39.215 --> 00:43:43.854
MICHAEL: Right now,
the snow is coming back
and the deep dark winter

00:43:43.887 --> 00:43:45.889
is coming upon us.

00:43:45.922 --> 00:43:48.392
 ♪ We are the hunted ♪♪

00:43:48.425 --> 00:43:52.029
BRITTNEY: We're the next
generation of Alaskans.

00:43:52.062 --> 00:43:54.364
Our children are the future.

00:43:54.397 --> 00:43:56.933
 ♪ We are the chosen ones ♪♪

00:43:56.966 --> 00:43:59.670
ALEX: Alaska's definitely a
place for people who want to

00:43:59.703 --> 00:44:02.072
live in a continual challenge.

00:44:02.705 --> 00:44:05.409
Mother Nature doesn't quit,
she doesn't tire.

00:44:06.042 --> 00:44:08.779
And the challenge never ends.

